1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:03,168
Just twelve thousand people live on this
tiny island,

2
00:00:03,228 --> 00:00:05,503
and you can walk around it in an
afternoon.

3
00:00:06,330 --> 00:00:09,196
This is Nauru, known as the Pleasant
Island.

4
00:00:09,660 --> 00:00:10,805
Its capital is Yaren.

5
00:00:12,665 --> 00:00:16,593
About twelve thousand people live here, on
twenty-one square kilometers.

6
00:00:18,453 --> 00:00:22,338
They speak Nauruan and Kiribati. In
Nauruan,

7
00:00:22,398 --> 00:00:26,552
hello is Ekamowir omo. In Kiribati, it's
Mauri.

8
00:00:28,412 --> 00:00:29,872
And they pay with the Australian dollar.

9
00:00:32,033 --> 00:00:36,777
Some fun facts. And stay until the end,
for a mystery nobody ever solved.

10
00:00:38,267 --> 00:00:42,624
Nauru has no official capital. The
government sits in the Yaren district.

11
00:00:43,184 --> 00:00:46,822
A single road circles the whole island,
about nineteen kilometers long.

12
00:00:47,382 --> 00:00:51,806
In 2026, the country changed its official
name to Naoero.

13
00:00:52,500 --> 00:00:54,928
Now, a few surprising places.

14
00:00:56,300 --> 00:00:58,847
Anibare Bay is the island's best beach.

15
00:00:58,907 --> 00:01:02,457
Legend says the entrance to the spirit
island is here.

16
00:01:03,017 --> 00:01:06,833
Command Ridge is the highest point, just
sixty-five meters up,

17
00:01:06,893 --> 00:01:09,458
with old Japanese guns from World War Two.

18
00:01:10,018 --> 00:01:14,850
Buada Lagoon is a rare pocket of fresh
water, ringed by palm trees.

19
00:01:15,410 --> 00:01:19,528
Beneath the island, the Moqua caves hide
an underground lake.

20
00:01:20,088 --> 00:01:23,508
And Topside, the rocky plateau left by
phosphate mining,

21
00:01:23,568 --> 00:01:25,123
looks like another planet.

22
00:01:25,800 --> 00:01:28,411
So, what does $10 get you here?

23
00:01:29,933 --> 00:01:31,491
Nauru uses the Australian dollar,

24
00:01:31,551 --> 00:01:34,187
so ten US dollars is about fourteen
Australian dollars.

25
00:01:34,247 --> 00:01:35,608
Let's see what that gets you.

26
00:01:36,168 --> 00:01:39,813
First, a ride from the airport to your
hotel: about three dollars fifty.

27
00:01:39,873 --> 00:01:41,151
That leaves you six fifty.

28
00:01:41,711 --> 00:01:46,323
Next, a litre of bottled water: also about
three fifty, more than back home,

29
00:01:46,383 --> 00:01:50,010
because almost everything is shipped in.
Three dollars left.

30
00:01:50,570 --> 00:01:54,838
A plate of chow mein at a Chinese
restaurant is about seven dollars,

31
00:01:54,898 --> 00:01:56,810
so that's already out of reach.

32
00:01:57,370 --> 00:02:00,208
A rental car for the day: about fifty-five
dollars.

33
00:02:00,768 --> 00:02:03,606
And one night at the Menen Hotel, the
biggest on the island:

34
00:02:03,666 --> 00:02:05,297
about a hundred and twenty dollars.

35
00:02:05,857 --> 00:02:09,202
So with ten dollars, you get a ride from
the airport, a bottle of water,

36
00:02:09,262 --> 00:02:10,810
and about three dollars left over.

37
00:02:11,467 --> 00:02:13,408
Now, the don't list.

38
00:02:14,867 --> 00:02:19,325
Don't wait for a taxi or a bus. There
aren't any. Ask your hotel for a ride,

39
00:02:19,385 --> 00:02:20,871
or rent a car for the day.

40
00:02:21,431 --> 00:02:25,206
Don't count on your bank card. Cards are
rarely accepted here,

41
00:02:25,266 --> 00:02:27,310
so bring Australian cash with you.

42
00:02:27,870 --> 00:02:32,591
Don't swim out past the reef. The currents
are strong, there are no lifeguards,

43
00:02:32,651 --> 00:02:33,862
and help is far away.

44
00:02:34,422 --> 00:02:37,647
Don't wear beachwear in town. People here
dress conservatively,

45
00:02:37,707 --> 00:02:39,472
so keep the swimsuit for the beach.

46
00:02:40,032 --> 00:02:42,517
And don't fly in to report the news on a
whim.

47
00:02:42,577 --> 00:02:46,445
Just applying for a media visa costs eight
thousand Australian dollars,

48
00:02:46,505 --> 00:02:48,880
and you don't get it back if you're
refused.

49
00:02:49,467 --> 00:02:50,828
Let's talk about culture.

50
00:02:52,333 --> 00:02:57,053
The national dish is coconut fish: raw
tuna in coconut cream and lime.

51
00:02:57,913 --> 00:03:00,184
Little traditional clothing survives,

52
00:03:00,244 --> 00:03:04,404
but islanders still weave pandanus leaves
into mats, hats and fans.

53
00:03:05,264 --> 00:03:07,949
Songs and dances, like the frigatebird
dance,

54
00:03:08,009 --> 00:03:10,145
are still performed at celebrations.

55
00:03:11,100 --> 00:03:12,186
Time for a little history.

56
00:03:13,700 --> 00:03:14,299
In 1900,

57
00:03:14,359 --> 00:03:21,796
a prospector found that a rock used as a
doorstop in a Sydney office was full of
phosphate.

58
00:03:21,856 --> 00:03:26,656
It came from Nauru, and mining began on
the island in 1906.

59
00:03:27,216 --> 00:03:31,678
In 1970, the island took control of its
own phosphate mining. For a time,

60
00:03:31,738 --> 00:03:36,200
that money made it one of the richest
countries in the world, per person.

61
00:03:36,760 --> 00:03:40,092
In 2002, the phosphate industry collapsed.
By then,

62
00:03:40,152 --> 00:03:45,346
Australia had signed a deal to run an
offshore processing centre on the island,

63
00:03:45,406 --> 00:03:47,940
and it became a major source of income.

64
00:03:48,600 --> 00:03:53,048
And now, the chapter you were all waiting
for: legends and mysteries.

65
00:03:54,433 --> 00:03:58,483
One Nauruan legend tells of Eigigu, a
young girl. In the story,

66
00:03:58,543 --> 00:04:02,658
a giant tree grows all the way up to the
sky. She ends up there,

67
00:04:02,718 --> 00:04:07,029
and becomes the wife of the moon. Nauruans
say that on some nights,

68
00:04:07,089 --> 00:04:09,573
you can still see the moon holding her.

69
00:04:10,133 --> 00:04:14,571
In the beginning, say the old stories,
there was only the sea,

70
00:04:14,631 --> 00:04:19,650
and a spider named Areop Enap. He found a
giant clam and crept inside,

71
00:04:19,710 --> 00:04:23,713
but it was dark. Two snails became the
moon and the sun,

72
00:04:23,773 --> 00:04:28,647
and a worm named Rigi pushed the shell
open. The top became the sky,

73
00:04:28,707 --> 00:04:33,073
the bottom became the earth, and Rigi's
sweat became the sea.

74
00:04:33,633 --> 00:04:36,743
The mystery: where did the Nauruan people
come from?

75
00:04:36,803 --> 00:04:40,218
The island was settled at least three
thousand years ago.

76
00:04:40,278 --> 00:04:44,608
The people are a mix of Micronesian,
Melanesian and Polynesian ancestry.

77
00:04:44,668 --> 00:04:48,510
Their language is unique, and gives few
hints about its origins.

78
00:04:48,570 --> 00:04:52,899
Nobody knows for sure who the first
settlers were, or how they got here.

79
00:04:55,800 --> 00:05:00,255
That was Nauru. Which country should we
visit next? Tell me in the comments.

